<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PMD ruleset — Codacy toggle, ON.

  NAME COLLISION, READ FIRST. This is a PMD ruleset, which is what a bare
  `ruleset.xml` at a repo root means by convention. It is NOT a GitHub
  repository ruleset — those are branch and tag protection rules, they are
  JSON, they are configured in the repository settings UI or via the API, and
  nothing in a file named ruleset.xml has any effect on them. The vault holds
  reference notes on GitHub rulesets ("GitHub Docs - About rulesets.md",
  "Creating rulesets for a repository.md"); this file is unrelated to those and
  changing it will not protect a branch.

  The two categories referenced below ship inside PMD itself — no plugin, no
  download, nothing to resolve.

  REACH TODAY IS ZERO. PMD analyses Java, Apex and a few other JVM-family
  languages; this repo's code is Python, shell and Markdown. These rules match
  nothing and cost nothing. They are armed for the first Java file rather than
  switched on after one exists, which is the useful order — the rules people
  resent most are the ones applied to code that is already written.

  Two categories, not five. `errorprone` and `bestpractices` are the ones that
  find defects. `codestyle` and `design` are largely taste, and `documentation`
  demands Javadoc on everything; adding them to an empty repo would guarantee
  that the first Java file ever committed arrives buried in complaints about
  brace placement.

  To take a single rule back out, keep the category ref and exclude by name:

    <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml">
      <exclude name="MissingSerialVersionUID"/>
    </rule>

  If you find yourself excluding half a category, the category was the wrong
  starting point — reference the individual rules instead.
-->
<ruleset name="idaho-vault"
         xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0 https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

  <description>
    Defect-finding baseline for IDAHO-VAULT: PMD's built-in errorprone and
    bestpractices categories. Matches nothing today — this repo has no Java.
    Not a GitHub repository ruleset; see the comment above.
  </description>

  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml"/>
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml"/>

</ruleset>
